          There is no objective, and the single step is an empty placeholder: empty
          description, empty test data, empty expected result (ck.db `zephyr_cases`,
          `has_objective = 0`).  The case title is the only authored authority.  No STEP
          heading below can quote authored text.  The seven steps were designed for this
          run from three sources: the case title, the "X960 platform support TPS" row 241
          (Dynamic configuration (Change, Delete), Supported: Yes), and the DUT's own CLI
          surface, read with `?` before anything was configured.
          The requirement behind TPS row 241 is at TPS line 894, verbatim:
            "Support a Dynamic configuration. Even during in operation, addition, change
             and deletion of a configuration must be effected dynamically unless a chip
             set or another hardware component requires restart..."
          Read the requirement carefully, because it asks for more than the title.
          1. It names THREE operations: addition, change AND deletion.  The title names
             only two.  All three are measured below.
          2. The key words are "during in operation" and "effected dynamically".  So the
             thing under test is not that a command is accepted.  It is that the running
             system's BEHAVIOUR changes, with no restart, while it is running.
          3. The only escape clause is "unless a chip set or another hardware component
             requires restart".  This log records whether any operation needed a restart.
