tb277# tools/tb ckreload tb277 ntp1021 u2 115200 240
  >>> reload   (04:53:40 UTC)
  reboot question: 'reboot system? (y/n):' -> y
  login seen after 60 s

  The leg was settled before it was read.  The first ping to .70 after the boot returned
  1 of 4, 75 percent packet loss.  The second returned 4 of 4.  Only then was the leg
  taken, 22 s after the login prompt, with NOTHING typed into the DUT:

  cap t192-afterboot-eth4.pcap: 22 frame(s)
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.66   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.65 -> 10.38.22.70   [ours]
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.70 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .70 still answers
    x4   ICMP t3/c1 10.38.22.70 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .66 unreachable, FROM .70
    x3   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.66 tell 10.38.22.70
    x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.70 tell 10.38.22.70       (duplicate-address probe at boot)
    x1   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.65 tell 10.38.22.70

  <-- Row for row the same as the leg before the reload.  The source of the unreachable
      is still .70, so the whole layer-3 identity came back, not only the address line.
  <-- The extra `who-has 10.38.22.70 tell 10.38.22.70` is the interface's own
      duplicate-address probe at boot.  It shows the interface was brought up from the
      saved configuration, rather than simply never torn down.

  Put back:

awplus(config)#interface vlan48
awplus(config-if)#ip address 10.38.22.66/27
awplus#show ip interface brief
  vlan48   10.38.22.66/27   admin up  running
  cap t192-restored-eth4.pcap: 21 frame(s)
    x4   ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .66 answers again
    x4   ICMP t3/c1 10.38.22.66 -> 10.38.22.65   [ours]     <- .70 unreachable, FROM .66
    x3   ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.70 tell 10.38.22.66

  => STEP 7 PASS: a change applied while the device was operating is saved and restored
     like any other configuration.  The arc on the wire is: .66 -> .70 -> (cold boot) ->
     .70 -> .66.  So "dynamic" on this platform does not mean "temporary".
